

NBA CONFERENCE/DIVISION STRUCTURE

30 teams
2 conferences, 15 teams per conference. east and west
6 divisions, 3 per conference, 5 teams per divisions. 

divisions by conference
east: atlantic, central, southeast
west: northwest, pacific, southeast

________________________________________________________________________

BASKETBALL JARGON

"box score" - table of stats from a game, lists things like points
rebounds, assists, steaks, blocks, etc. for teams/players

"field goal" - any normal shot from the floor, either 2/3 points
not including free throws but including layups, dunks, midrange
jumpers, 3-pointers

________________________________________________________________________

DATA EXPLANATIONS

each row in games df is from the home team's pov, so "win" refers to
if the home team won, 1=home win, 0=away win
each row is mirrored to also be from the away team's pov

"plus_minus" stat means how much the team outscored the opponent in
that game
plus_minus = team_points - opponent_points, and since each row is
mirrored, So if BOS beats NYK 112-104:
BOS row: PLUS_MINUS = +8
NYK row: PLUS_MINUS = -8

________________________________________________________________________

MODEL EXPLANATIONS

"rolling window" - window for which avg stats from prior games should
be calculated for a team, a tunable parameter
used because team's performance is temporal, recency matters most

"min period" - early in the season, teams may not have rolling_window
number of games to calculate averages from, so the min_periods param is
the minimum acceptable value where we can calculate averages 

example: rolling_window=10, min_periods=5
early in the season 
previous 0-4 games: rolling average = NaN, skip this game for training
previous 5-9 games: average however many previous games exist
previous 10+ games: average previous 10 games

________________________________________________________________________

GENERAL THINGS LEARNED

___________________________________________

TEMPORAL CV

with temporal data, train/test split should not be shuffled
so the older data should be train, and newer data be test
because this also mirrors what the model will do during inference
also random shuffle can leak future information into the past

also temporal cv trains with each new fold, so fold n uses the 
data from folds 1 to n-1, which makes sense why accuracy tends
to increase as folds do, more data to train on.
but temporal cv still useful for seeing if diff "eras" had 
larger difficulty predicting, distribution shift, etc

___________________________________________

BRIER SCORE: 

brier score is standard for probabilistic, temporal prediction like
in contexts such as sports prediction as it a
sum of (predicted_probability - actual_result)^2, so want to minimize this

___________________________________________

ROC/AUC:

measures ranking ability, if in game A, home wins, and game B, home loses
then good model should usually say P(home wins Game A) > P(home wins Game B)

AUC asks, across many pairs of one positive example and one negative example:
How often did the positive one receive the higher score?
where positive = home_win = 1, negative = home_lose = 0

So ROC/AUC is saying:
Do actual home wins tend to receive higher predicted 
probabilities than actual home losses?

ROC Axes
x-axis: FPR = false positive rate
y-axis: TPR = true positive rate

TPR, true positive rate:
Of games where the home team actually won,
what fraction did we correctly predict as home wins?
Also called recall/sensitivity.

FPR, false positive rate:
Of games where the home team actually lost,
what fraction did we incorrectly predict as home wins?
So each threshold gives one point:

(threshold -> FPR, TPR)
A good model gets high TPR without high FPR.

Bad/random model:
as TPR goes up, FPR goes up at about the same rate
AUC around 0.50

Good model:
TPR rises faster than FPR
AUC above 0.50

Why Use TPR/FPR?
Because they show the tradeoff between:
catching actual home wins
vs
mistakenly calling home wins when home team loses
Threshold 0.50 is only one possible tradeoff. ROC/AUC checks whether 
the model’s probability ordering is useful across all possible tradeoffs.


___________________________________________

LOG LOSS 

ml setups use log base e remember

if actual = 1:
    loss = -log(predicted_probability)
    
if actual = 0:
    loss = -log(1 - predicted_probability)

so good guesses makes for -log(close to 1) = small loss
and bad guesses makes for -log(close to 0) = big loss

x       -log(x)
0.0     infinity / undefined
0.1     2.3026
0.2     1.6094
0.3     1.2040
0.4     0.9163
0.5     0.6931
0.6     0.5108
0.7     0.3567
0.8     0.2231
0.9     0.1054
1.0     0.0000

since -log(1)=0, -log(0)=infinity, -log(0.5)=.693 and between 
(0,1] -log(x) shrinkage looks quadratic-ish as input grows 

as u go from x=1 to x=0, y grows faster, eventually shoots straight
up to infinity, displaying an increasing penalty as pred strays 
further from actual the loss will grow faster and faster, to penalize
the model being more confident in the wrong direction

as input of -log(x) grows, output shrinks
conditions make it so that ideal output will shrink the -log if 
pred close to actual

across all games, sklearn does mean(loss_1, loss_2, loss_3, ...)

each game gets probability, but what about many games, as we predict:
For many games, the model assigns probabilities to each actual outcome:

0.80, 0.60, 0.75, 0.55, ...
The likelihood is the product, want high likelihood (high when what your
model says alligns with reality, so want to maximize)

0.80 * 0.60 * 0.75 * 0.55 * ...
Products of many probabilities get tiny and annoying. Logs turn products into sums:

log(a * b * c) = log(a) + log(b) + log(c)
So instead of maximizing product likelihood, we maximize sum log likelihood.

but log((0,1])) =  negative number, so "maximizing" means make less negative,
confusing and weird, so -log() makes all outputs positive, and minimizing is easy
just want entire quantity closer to 0

so maximizing the log likelihood = minimizing the negative log likelihood
which is why the -log(x) exists and can be used, minimizing -log is same 
as maximizing log likelihood, since log is always increasing, same as
maximizing likelihood 








